5cdcd959 (1114 1793351163718 "guenchi" (("4gm4c2jr" . 3240) ("w5vbequy" . 14982)) (put ((kind . section) (title . "landing f392") (src . "working: a fold that fails at a named cut is answered in its own class, not as an unusable cut\n\nwrite --based-on folded the historical reduction at the named cut\ninside a catch-all guard, so every raise from that fold (the store's\nlock held past the daemon's budget, an unreadable segment, a log error)\nbecame #f and was answered invalid-working-baseline cut-unusable, or\nno-text-for-version at working-restore!, or a flattened \"Working\nstorage failed\" at state-at-cut-text. A caller whose cut was fine went\nlooking for what was wrong with it while the store was only busy.\n\nworking.sc gains fold-at-cut: the fold runs under one guard that\nreturns a fold-failure record carrying the failure's own answer\n(classify-failure, else commit-failure's text; a lock busy past the\nbudget is still (error store-busy (path ...))). The four sites that\nfolded at a cut (working-write!'s historical reduction and the\ncommitted parent, working-restore!, state-at-cut-text) render the\nrecord where they answered the unusable cut, and every refusal that\ncame before still comes first. A cut that does not parse or names a\nstate that cannot be rebuilt stays cut-unusable; parse-cut now runs\noutside the guard and answers #f for any text.\n\nTests: test/fold-failure.sc (19 rows: each of the four sites under a\nbusy lock, an unreadable segment and a log error answers the class,\nthe unusable cut still answers cut-unusable, and the refusals' order).\ntest/unreadable-census.sexp pins the one handler that replaced three.\nrows-baseline follows the new fixture. README states the answers.\n") (parent . root) (ord . 542))))
